The “never query after save” rule is an optimization guideline with this explicit exception. Do not substitute `UPSERT`: even a technical self-assignment is an UPDATE and may fire database triggers.

```kotlin
val result = sqlClient.save(incoming) {
    setMode(SaveMode.UPSERT)
    setVersionMode(VersionMode.ASSIGNMENT)
    setUpdateWhere(CatalogRevision::class) {
        newNonNull(CatalogRevision::version) gt table.version
    }
}

if (result.isAccepted) {
    consume(result.modifiedEntity)
} else {
    // Normal rejection: an existing row has an equal or newer revision.
    handleRejectedRevision(incoming)
}
```

Here `incoming` is the supplied `CatalogRevision`, with its ID and non-null external `@Version` value loaded.

- **`UPSERT`** inserts a missing ID. The update predicate applies only to existing rows; it does not prevent insertion.
- **`VersionMode.ASSIGNMENT`** writes the supplied revision directly, without Jimmer’s implicit optimistic-lock check or version increment.
- **`setUpdateWhere`** permits updates only when the incoming revision is strictly greater. Equal or older revisions produce a normal rejected result, rather than an optimistic-lock exception. Check **`isAccepted`**, not affected-row counts or the apparent contents of `modifiedEntity`.
- **Version mode is root-only:** this configuration does **not** propagate to child objects.

Concurrency guarantees depend on the dialect: native conditional upsert is dialect-dependent; a select-then-write fallback requires appropriate transaction isolation or locking. With this update condition, use id-only/null owning-side references rather than mutable referenced objects.

