**Concurrency caveat:** the insert attempt and conflict lookup are separate statements, not an atomic “insert-or-return-existing.” A concurrent deletion or SKU change can remove the conflicting row before lookup; snapshot isolation can also affect visibility. Use an explicit transaction/isolation policy and bounded retries—retry the whole transaction when a fresh snapshot is required. Do not assume the lookup must succeed.

Do **not** substitute `UPSERT` merely to retrieve the ID: even `SET column = column` is a real update and can fire database triggers, violating this contract.

```kotlin
import org.babyfish.jimmer.sql.ast.mutation.SaveMode
import org.babyfish.jimmer.sql.ast.mutation.VersionMode

// incoming has its ID and external @Version value loaded.
val result = sqlClient.save(incoming) {
    setMode(SaveMode.UPSERT)
    setVersionMode(VersionMode.ASSIGNMENT)
    setUpdateWhere(CatalogRevision::class) {
        newNonNull(CatalogRevision::version) gt table.version
    }
}

if (result.isAccepted) {
    // Missing ID inserted, or existing row updated to a newer revision.
    consume(result.modifiedEntity)
} else {
    // Equal/older revision: normal rejection; do not consume as a write.
    handleRejectedRevision(incoming)
}
```

- **`UPSERT`** inserts missing IDs. The update condition applies only to existing rows, so it does not prevent insertion.
- **`ASSIGNMENT`** writes the supplied external revision directly, without implicit version checking or incrementing. **`setUpdateWhere`** rejects equal/older revisions normally rather than raising an optimistic-lock exception. Check `isAccepted`, not affected-row counts.
- **Version mode is root-only:** it does **not** propagate to child objects.

For concurrent ingestion, verify the dialect uses native conditional upsert; a select-then-write fallback needs explicit isolation/locking to preserve the newer-revision-only guarantee. With this update condition, use id-only or null owning-side references rather than mutable associated objects.

Put `@OnDissociate(DissociateAction.DELETE)` on **`Bin.warehouse()`**, the owning to-one reference—not on the inverse `Warehouse.bins()` collection.

```java
@Entity
public interface Warehouse {
    // Other properties omitted
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "warehouse")
    List<Bin> bins();
}

@Entity
public interface Bin {
    // Other properties omitted
    @ManyToOne
    @OnDissociate(DissociateAction.DELETE)
    Warehouse warehouse();
}
```

**No FK change is required.** Keep the existing database `RESTRICT` policy unless deleting a warehouse itself should have different behavior.

- **ORM dissociation:** When saving a loaded `bins` collection with replacement semantics (`AssociatedSaveMode.REPLACE`), Jimmer deletes existing `Bin` rows omitted from that collection. It does not delete the warehouse or set the non-null FK to null. An unloaded collection does not request replacement.
- **Database FK behavior:** `ON DELETE CASCADE` deletes referencing bins when the **warehouse row itself is deleted**. `RESTRICT` prevents that parent deletion while referencing bins remain. Neither policy implements collection replacement.

The annotation controls Jimmer’s dissociation operation; it neither requires nor installs a cascading database FK.

